response:
 The number of feet in one mile (5280 feet) divided by the length of the track gives the number of times Joneal circles the track $5280 \div 50 = 105.6$.  The modular arithmetic of this problem means that we only have to look at the fractional part of this quotient to determine how far Joneal is past point $S$ after running one mile. Since $105.6 = 105 + 0.6$, we know that Joneal has run past point $S$ for $0.6\times 50 = 30$ feet. Dividing 30 by 50 again, $30\div 50=0.6$, we find that Joneal is 60% of the way from $S$ along the track. Since quarter lengths are division by 4, 60% of the way along a quarter is halfway along the quarter, i.e., the boundary between quarter $B$ and quarter $C$.  Therefore, Joneal stopped in quarter \dots \dots래 de \dots香气 => C \dots disse

